</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Evaluation of MLLM Judges for UIs</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the efficacy of MLLMs in assessing UIs by comparing their judgments to human evaluations collected through a crowdsourcing study (<a href="#S3" title="3. Study Methodology ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Our analysis proceeds in two parts.
In <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Task 1: Absolute Score Prediction ‣ 4. Evaluation of MLLM Judges for UIs ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we examine how well MLLMs predict absolute human scores (prompt shown in <a href="#A2.F7" title="In Appendix B Additional Results &amp; Prompts ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), assessing both the accuracy of these predictions and their utility for estimating user preferences.
In <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Task 2: Pairwise Preference Prediction ‣ 4. Evaluation of MLLM Judges for UIs ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we analyze pairwise comparisons of UIs based on human data, and evaluate how closely MLLM predictions of these comparisons align with human preferences (prompt shown in <a href="#A2.F8" title="In Appendix B Additional Results &amp; Prompts ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
The analysis is conducted over all UI factors in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.2. Factors ‣ 3. Study Methodology ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To avoid bias, and ensure generality and robustness of our findings, we leverage three diverse MLLMs, including GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, and Llama<span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>3.2<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>11B<span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>Vision.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Task 1: Absolute Score Prediction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct three studies evaluating how closely MLLMs’ responses to UIs resemble those of humans.
In <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1. Individual Scores ‣ 4.1. Task 1: Absolute Score Prediction ‣ 4. Evaluation of MLLM Judges for UIs ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a>, we first analyze MLLM and human scores separately. Results suggest that MLLMs are mostly well aligned to humans, with a few factors where they may significantly under or over predict human scores.
In <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="4.1.2. Score Prediction ‣ 4.1. Task 1: Absolute Score Prediction ‣ 4. Evaluation of MLLM Judges for UIs ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.2</span></a>, we analyze prediction errors of MLLMs using four metrics: MSE, MAE, accuracy, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy.
Results suggest that MLLMs well-predict human responses, with Claude being the best judge overall. MLLMs on average overestimated factors like <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F9D58;">Ease of Use</span>. While consistently underestimating factors like <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#DB4437;">Aesthetic Pleasure</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#DB4437;">Interesting</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#DB4437;">Comfort</span>. Meanwhile, models showed they could reasonably predict scores (within 0.50 Likert points) for factors such as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F9D58;">Clarity</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F9D58;">Visual Hierarchy</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4285F4;">Memorable</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4285F4;">Trustworthy</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4285F4;">Intuitive</span>.
Finally in <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS3" title="4.1.3. Ranking Prediction ‣ 4.1. Task 1: Absolute Score Prediction ‣ 4. Evaluation of MLLM Judges for UIs ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.3</span></a>, we analyze how well MLLM scores predict the ranking of UIs by humans. The factor scan be well predicted, and the best judge overall is Llama.
To reduce the randomness in our analysis due to random MLLM scores, we evaluate each UI and factor by each MLLM <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> times and then take the median score. Our MLLM prompt is reported in <a href="#A2.F7" title="In Appendix B Additional Results &amp; Prompts ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Individual Scores</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by comparing individual human and MLLM scores.
For each UI and factor, we report averaged human scores and median MLLM scores.
These averages represent an average perception of a given UI in a given factor.
We also included average scores from humans and MLLMs aggregated by the three domains and all UIs, see <a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3.4. Result of the Human Data Collection ‣ 3. Study Methodology ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We make several observations. First, MLLM judges align reasonably well with humans. As an example, note the average human and MLLM scores over all factors and UIs in <a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3.4. Result of the Human Data Collection ‣ 3. Study Methodology ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (last four rows and last column).
The human score is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.10</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas Claude predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.08</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.08</annotation></semantics></math>, GPT-4o predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.75</annotation></semantics></math>, and Llama predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.98</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, all MLLM scores are within <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.35</annotation></semantics></math> of the human scores. Since this is significantly less than the range of our <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>-point Likert scale, we conclude that MLLMs reasonably mimic human scores.
Second, MLLM scores are often closer to each other than those of humans, as indicated by smaller standard deviations of the scores in <a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3.4. Result of the Human Data Collection ‣ 3. Study Methodology ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We note this specifically for Claude and Llama. GPT-4o has sometimes a larger standard deviation and sometimes smaller.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">When considering individual factors in <a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3.4. Result of the Human Data Collection ‣ 3. Study Methodology ‣ MLLM as a UI Judge: Benchmarking Multimodal LLMs for Predicting Human Perception of User Interfaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, MLLMs tend to perform well on cognitive and perceptual factors.</em> For instance, the human score for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4285F4;">Trustworthy</span> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.34</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.34</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas Claude predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.42</annotation></semantics></math>, GPT-4o predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.53" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.53</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.53</annotation></semantics></math>, and Llama predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.37</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.37</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the only factor where all MLLM scores are within <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> of the human score. MLLMs also perform well on <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F9D58;">Visual Hierarchy</span>, where all MLLM scores are within <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> of the human scores. The human score is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.23</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.23</annotation></semantics></math>, while Claude predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.37</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.37</annotation></semantics></math>, GPT-4o predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.23</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.23</annotation></semantics></math>, and Llama predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.70</annotation></semantics></math>. Overall, MLLMs likely approximate these scores effectively because the factors have concrete cues in the UI. These factors are correlated with layout, reassuring designs and phrases, and spacing; all of which MLLMs can gather from the UI screenshots.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MLLMs struggle with some emotional factors, such as <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#DB4437;">Interesting</span>.</em> Specifically, all MLLM scores of this factor underestimate human scores. The human score is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.82</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.82</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas Claude predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.10</annotation></semantics></math>, GPT-4o predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.73</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.73</annotation></semantics></math>, and Llama predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.50</annotation></semantics></math>. The MLLMs likely struggle with this and other factors like <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F9D58;">Ease of Use</span> because they are more difficult to quantify for MLLMs. Factors like ease of use are highly tied a users technical literacy and cannot be inferred from visual cues alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muzaffar et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton, 2012</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>We report seven error metrics for all factors and MLLM judges. Four metrics measure the ability to predict scores (MSE, MAE, accuracy, and <math id="S4.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy) and three measure their utility for ranking (Pearson correlation coefficient, Spearman’s <math id="S4.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, and Kendall’s <math id="S4.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>). The last row contains metric values for all factors jointly. Higher correlation coefficients than <math id="S4.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4</annotation></semantics></math> are reported in bold.</figcaption>
